%T A400609 4,2,10,5,5,6,8,9,2,7,3,4,2,10,11,6,2,3,4,18,12,2,3,4,6,10,5,8,9,2,2,
%U A400609 3,4,11,7,6,26,26,2,10,13,14,3,4,2,6
%N A400609 Integers r occurring in solutions of m^2 = p! * q! * r!, with p > q > r > 1, ordered by increasing m (A400208).
%C A400609 This sequence gives the corresponding values of r for the solutions to Problem 1, Round Two, of the 2023 New Zealand Mathematical Olympiad, which asks whether there exist infinitely many triples of integers p > q > r > 1 such that p! * q! * r! is a perfect square m^2.
%C A400609 Terms a(1) = 3 and a(9) = 4 come from the family (m, p, q, r) = ((t!)!, t!, t!-1, t), t >= 3. Here t = 3 and t = 4, respectively.
%C A400609 The family (p, q, r) = (k! * x^2, k! * x^2 - 1, k), k > 1, x >= 1, gives infinitely many solutions.
%C A400609 -> For r = 2, taking x = 2, 3, 4, 5 gives a(3) = 2, a(6) = 2, a(14) = 2, a(21) = 2.
%C A400609 -> For r = 3, taking x = 1, 2 gives a(1) = 3 and a(8) = 3.
%H A400609 New Zealand Mathematical Olympiad Committee, <a href="https://www.mathsolympiad.org.nz/competitions/nzmo/files/problems.php?round=2&amp;year=2023&amp;type=solutions">2023 NZMO Round 2, problem 1.
%H A400609 <a href="/index/O#Olympiads">Index to sequences related to Olympiads and other Mathematical Competitions</a>.
%e A400609 For a(4) = 2, we have 9! * 7! * 2 = 60480^2 = A400208(4)^2.
%Y A400609 Cf. A400208 (m), A400607 (p), A400608 (q), this sequence (r).
%K A400609 nonn,more,new
%O A400609 1,1
